    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S2075A\n\nSPONSOR: ADDABBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to certain school holi-\ndays; and to repeal certain provisions of such law relating thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo allow the designation of Diwali as a school holiday in New York city.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 repeals\n\nSection 2586 of the education law.\n\nSection 2 amends Subdivision 2-a of section 3635 of the education law,\nas amended by chapter 424 of the laws of 2005.\n\nSection 3 sets the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nClick hereto enter text.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York City public schools customarily close for holidays observed by\nlarge numbers of students, parents, and faculty. Over 200,000 Hindu,\nSikh, Jain, and Buddhist New York City residents observe the festival of\nDiwali. Designating Diwali as a school holiday in New York City would\nprovide the many New Yorkers observing the day with the same accommo-\ndation afforded those who observe other holidays such as Lunar New Year,\nEid alFitr, Rosh Hashanah, and Christmas. New York City Mayor Eric\nAdams, Schools Chancellor David Banks, and the Department of Education\nsupport this bill's framework for designation of Diwali as a school\nholiday. This bill rescinds the school holiday of Anniversary Day, so\nthat Diwali will become a holiday without any effect on session length.\nSchools Chancellor Banks noted that Anniversary Day is \"the perfect\nexample...of a day that we could substitute...for something much more\nmeaningful.\"\n\nSection 2586 of the Education Law requires that Anniversary Day be a\nschool holiday in Brooklyn and Queens, and New York City public schools\nin all boroughs now close for this holiday. Anniversary Day is an anti-\nquated day rarely observed, which can be rescinded to accommodate the\ndesignation of Diwali as a school holiday. After passage of this bill,\nthe New York City Department of Education will be free to designate\nDiwali as a public school holiday, making history for a growing communi-\nty of new Americans in New York City.\n\n \nAMENDMENTS:\n\nThe A-pint only includes original bill sections 2, 3 and 4.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of July next succeeding the day\non which it shall have become a law.\n",
